# Modifying & Deleting Properties ✏️

Objects in JavaScript are mutable even when declared with `const`:

```javascript
const project = { title: "Quiz Platform", status: "draft" };

// Add or update property
project.status = "published";
project.stars = 42;

// Delete a property
delete project.title;

console.log(project);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const project = { title: "Quiz Platform", status: "draft" };

project.status = "published";
project.stars = 42;
delete project.title;

console.log("Modified project object:", project);
console.log("Has stars property?", "stars" in project);
FULL_CODE_END -->

> 💡 **`const` vs Mutability:** Declaring an object with `const` prevents reassigning the variable to a *new* object, but its internal properties can still be modified!

# Object Methods & `this` ⚙️

Functions stored as properties inside an object are called **methods**:

```javascript
const car = {
  brand: "Tesla",
  model: "Model 3",
  batteryLevel: 85,
  drive(miles) {
    this.batteryLevel -= Math.round(miles * 0.3);
    console.log(`${this.brand} ${this.model} drove ${miles} miles. Battery: ${this.batteryLevel}%`);
  }
};

car.drive(50);
```

