# Cloning Objects: Shallow vs Deep 🧬

## 1. Shallow Copy with Spread or `Object.assign`:
```javascript
const original = { a: 1, b: 2 };
const clone = { ...original };
clone.a = 99;

console.log("Original a:", original.a); // 1 (Unaffected!)
console.log("Clone a:", clone.a);       // 99
```

> ⚠️ **Shallow Copy Warning:** Nested objects are copied by reference! Modifying a nested object inside `clone` will mutate the original unless you make a deep copy with `structuredClone(obj)`.

# Deep Cloning with `structuredClone()` 🚀

Modern JavaScript includes native deep cloning:

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Leo",
  address: { city: "Berlin", zip: 10115 }
};

// True deep copy!
const deepCopiedUser = structuredClone(user);
deepCopiedUser.address.city = "Munich";

console.log("Original City:", user.address.city);       // "Berlin" (Safe!)
console.log("Clone City:", deepCopiedUser.address.city); // "Munich"
```

